<DocsLayout
  title="Getting Started"
  description="Structure-D in 5 minutes — from installation to your first extraction."
  toc={toc}
  editPath="/docs/getting-started.astro"
  next={{ label: 'Installation', href: `${base}docs/installation` }}
>
  <h2 id="what-is-structure-d">What is Structure-D?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Structure-D</strong> is a modular pipeline framework that converts unstructured documents
    (PDFs, images, HTML, DOCX, and more) into <strong>validated, schema-constrained structured data</strong> using LLMs.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is designed around three principles:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Format-focused, not domain-specific</strong> — Structure-D handles file formats, not document types. You define what you want to extract.</li>
    <li><strong>Schema-driven</strong> — Your Pydantic model is the contract. Structure-D guarantees the output matches it.</li>
    <li><strong>Async-first</strong> — Every I/O operation is <code>async</code>. Process thousands of documents in parallel.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout info">
    <span class="callout-icon">ℹ️</span>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <p class="callout-title">Currently in beta</p>
      <p class="callout-content">Structure-D v0.2.0 is production-ready for Phase 1 features. ETL scheduling and HITL workflows are on the roadmap for v0.3.0.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
  <p>Every document flows through a six-stage pipeline:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Ingestion</strong> — a format-appropriate parser extracts raw text, tables, and images from your file</li>
    <li><strong>Preprocessing</strong> — text is normalized, cleaned, and chunked into optimal-size segments</li>
    <li><strong>Model Routing</strong> — the router selects the best registered model for your task</li>
    <li><strong>Inference</strong> — the LLM provider generates structured output constrained to your schema</li>
    <li><strong>Validation</strong> — every output is validated against the Pydantic model; invalid outputs are retried</li>
    <li><strong>Storage</strong> — results are written to JSONL, CSV, or a database destination</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="quick-start">Quick start</h2>
  <p>Install Structure-D with the extras you need:</p>

  <div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header">
      <span class="code-block-lang">bash</span>
      <button class="code-block-copy">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">pip install "structure-d[ingestion,api,llm]"</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>Then extract structured data from any document:</p>

  <div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header">
      <span class="code-block-filename">quickstart.py</span>
      <span class="code-block-lang">python</span>
      <button class="code-block-copy">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <pre><code class="language-python">import asyncio
from pathlib import Path
from pydantic import BaseModel
from structure_d.pipeline import Pipeline
from structure_d.inference.providers import OpenAIProvider

# 1. Define your extraction schema as a Pydantic model
class Invoice(BaseModel):
    vendor: str
    invoice_number: str
    total_amount: float
    line_items: list[str]
    due_date: str | None = None

# 2. Create the pipeline
async def main():
    pipeline = Pipeline(
        schema_cls=Invoice,
        provider=OpenAIProvider(),   # or AnthropicProvider(), OllamaProvider(), VLLMProvider()
    )

    # 3. Run on a file — format is auto-detected
    results = await pipeline.run(Path("invoice.pdf"))

    for result in results:
        print(result.structured_output)
        # → &#123;"vendor": "Acme Corp", "invoice_number": "INV-001", "total_amount": 1240.00, ...&#125;

asyncio.run(main())</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    Structure-D automatically detects the file format, selects the appropriate parser,
    chunks the content, runs inference, and validates the output — retrying up to 3 times
    if the model produces invalid JSON.
  </p>

  <div class="callout tip">
    <span class="callout-icon">💡</span>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <p class="callout-title">Use built-in schemas</p>
      <p class="callout-content">Don't want to define a schema? Use one of the built-ins: <code>key_value</code>, <code>table</code>, <code>entity</code>, <code>form</code>, <code>classification</code>, or <code>summary</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header">
      <span class="code-block-lang">python</span>
      <button class="code-block-copy">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <pre><code class="language-python">from structure_d.schemas.generic import get_schema

# Use a built-in schema by name
KeyValue = get_schema("key_value")
pipeline = Pipeline(schema_cls=KeyValue, provider=OpenAIProvider())</code></pre>
  </div>

